We can write the equation as $z^{12} = 2^6,$ so the solutions are of the form
\[z = \sqrt{2} \operatorname{cis} \frac{2 \pi k}{12},\]where $0 \le k \le 11.$  These solutions are equally spaced on the circle with radius $\sqrt{2}.$

[asy]
unitsize(1.5 cm);

int i;

draw(Circle((0,0),sqrt(2)));
draw((-2,0)--(2,0));
draw((0,-2)--(0,2));

for (i = 0; i <= 11; ++i) {
  dot(sqrt(2)*dir(30*i));
}

label("$\sqrt{2}$", (sqrt(2)/2,0), S);
[/asy]

Noting that the imaginary parts cancel due to symmetry, the sum of the solutions with positive real part is then
\begin{align*}
&\sqrt{2} \operatorname{cis} \left( -\frac{\pi}{3} \right) + \sqrt{2} \operatorname{cis} \left( -\frac{\pi}{6} \right) + \sqrt{2} \operatorname{cis} 0 + \sqrt{2} \operatorname{cis} \frac{\pi}{6} + \sqrt{2} \operatorname{cis} \frac{\pi}{3} \\
&= \sqrt{2} \cos \frac{\pi}{3} + \sqrt{2} \cos \frac{\pi}{6} + \sqrt{2} \cos 0 + \sqrt{2} \cos \frac{\pi}{6} + \sqrt{2} \cos \frac{\pi}{3} \\
&= \boxed{2 \sqrt{2} + \sqrt{6}}.
\end{align*}

The projection $\mathbf{P}$ takes $\begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{pmatrix}$ to $\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ y \\ z \end{pmatrix}.$

[asy]
import three;

size(180);
currentprojection = perspective(6,3,2);

triple I = (1,0,0), J = (0,1,0), K = (0,0,1), O = (0,0,0);
triple V = (2.2,2.5,2.5), W = (0,2.5,2.5);

draw(V--W,dashed);
draw(O--V, red, Arrow3(6));
draw(O--W,blue, Arrow3(6));
draw(O--3*I, Arrow3(6));
draw(O--3*J, Arrow3(6));
draw(O--3*K, Arrow3(6));

label("$x$", 3.2*I);
label("$y$", 3.2*J);
label("$z$", 3.2*K);
label("$\mathbf{v}$", V, NW);
label("$\mathbf{w}$", W, NE);
[/asy]

Thus,
\[\mathbf{P} \mathbf{i} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}, \quad \mathbf{P} \mathbf{j} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}, \quad \mathbf{P} \mathbf{k} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix},\]so
\[\mathbf{P} = \boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}}.\]

Let $\theta = \angle CBA.$  Since $\angle DBC = 2 \theta,$ $\angle DBA = 3 \theta.$

[asy]
unitsize(1 cm);

pair A, B, C, D;

A = (0,0);
B = (5,0);
C = (0,1);
D = (0,37/11);

draw(A--B--D---cycle);
draw(B--C);

label("$A$", A, SW);
label("$B$", B, SE);
label("$C$", C, W);
label("$D$", D, NW);
label("$1$", (A + C)/2, W);
label("$5$", (A + B)/2, S);
[/asy]

Note that $\tan \theta = \frac{1}{5}.$  By the triple angle formula,
\[\tan 3 \theta = \frac{3 \tan \theta - \tan^3 \theta}{1 - 3 \tan^2 \theta} = \frac{3 (\frac{1}{5}) - (\frac{1}{5})^3}{1 - 3 (\frac{1}{5})^2} = \frac{37}{55}.\]Therefore,
\[AD = AB \tan 3 \theta = 5 \cdot \frac{37}{55} = \boxed{\frac{37}{11}}.\]

inputs.problem:
Two circles with spinners at their centers are divided into three equal regions as shown. When both spinners are spun, what is the probability that the product of the two values is negative? Express your answer as a common fraction. [asy]
size(140);
void spinner(picture pic=currentpicture, real rotate, int a, int b, int c)
{
dot(pic,(0,0),linewidth(4));
draw(pic, unitcircle ^^ (0,0)--dir(90+rotate) ^^ (0,0)--dir(210+rotate) ^^ (0,0)--dir(-30+rotate));
label(pic, "$"+string(a)+"$",dir(30+rotate)/2,fontsize(10pt));
label(pic, "$"+string(b)+"$",dir(150+rotate)/2,fontsize(10pt));
label(pic, "$"+string(c)+"$",dir(-90+rotate)/2,fontsize(10pt));
}
picture p;
spinner(0,-1,-3,5); spinner(p,180,2,4,-6);
draw((0,0)--dir(-10)/2,EndArrow(4)); draw(p,(0,0)--dir(-75)/2,EndArrow(4)); add(shift(2.5*right)*p);[/asy]
